/* ---------- Easy Learn ---------- */
var PRIMER = [
  ["What a medication does", "Every drug has an indication (why you give it), contraindications (when you must not), and side effects (what else it does). Know all three before you give anything."],
  ["The rights of giving meds", "Right patient, right drug, right dose, right route, right time, and right documentation. Then reassess: did it work, and did anything bad happen?"],
  ["How fast routes work", "Fastest to slowest: IV and IO, then inhaled, then intranasal and IM, then sublingual, then oral. Oral is slowest because the gut and liver get it first."],
  ["Agonists and antagonists", "An agonist turns a receptor on (fentanyl turns on opioid receptors). An antagonist blocks it (naloxone blocks opioid receptors)."],
  ["Alpha and beta in one minute", "Alpha-1 squeezes blood vessels (BP goes up). Beta-1 is the heart: 1 heart (faster, stronger). Beta-2 is the lungs: 2 lungs (airways open). Epinephrine hits all three."],
  ["Sympathetic vs parasympathetic", "Sympathetic is fight or flight: fast heart, open airways, big pupils. Parasympathetic is rest and digest: slow heart, secretions, small pupils. Atropine blocks the rest-and-digest side."],
  ["Half-life", "The time for half the drug to leave the blood. Short half-life (adenosine, seconds) wears off fast. Long half-life (methadone, a day or more) can outlast your antidote."],
  ["Weight-based dosing", "Most kids' doses are per kilogram. Pounds divided by 2.2 equals kilograms. Multiply the dose per kg by the weight, then double-check the math out loud."],
  ["Antidote pairs to know cold", "Opioids: naloxone. Benzodiazepines: flumazenil (rarely used). Beta blockers: glucagon. Calcium channel blockers: calcium. Acetaminophen: N-acetylcysteine. Cyanide: hydroxocobalamin. Organophosphates: atropine and pralidoxime. Heparin: protamine. Warfarin: vitamin K."]
];
