  { test: /cephalosporin/i, text: "According to the 2022 AAAAI/ACAAI drug allergy practice parameter, cephalosporin cross-reactivity depends mostly on shared R1 side chains, not on the generation; studies show cross-reactivity with penicillins and carbapenems is low but not zero." },
  { test: /sulfonamide/i, text: "Studies show little immunologic cross-reactivity between sulfonamide antibiotics and non-antibiotic sulfonamides (Strom et al., N Engl J Med 2003); the drug labels still list sulfonamide hypersensitivity warnings." },
  { test: /NSAID|COX/i, text: "According to the AAAAI, people with NSAID-exacerbated respiratory disease often react to every COX-1 inhibitor; studies show COX-2 selective agents are usually tolerated in that group. Single-NSAID allergies can be drug-specific." },
  { test: /angiotensin-converting|ACE inhibitor/i, text: "Studies show a small risk of recurrent angioedema when an ARB follows ACE-inhibitor angioedema (Haymore et al., 2008 meta-analysis); ARB labels carry an angioedema warning." },
  { test: /opioid/i, text: "Studies show many opioid 'allergies' are histamine-release reactions (itching, flushing) rather than true IgE allergy; references describe structural classes (phenanthrenes, phenylpiperidines, diphenylheptanes) when reviewing cross-reactivity." },
  { test: /fluoroquinolone/i, text: "According to the 2022 AAAAI/ACAAI practice parameter, cross-reactivity among fluoroquinolones is possible and is not reliably predicted." }
